# murayama1962_table1.csv

Dataset: atmo-tsar — Pressure wave of the 30 October 1961 Novaya Zemlya explosion: three passages
  at eight Japanese microbarographs and Showa Base (Murayama 1962, Table 1)
File: data/atmo/tsar/transcription/murayama1962_table1.csv
SHA-256: 6a1a292b6bebf3406c2ef58b56e2252c7af545b75743d177569ccb45c0ce8b6d (also in
  data/atmo/tsar/inputs.lock; check with: cd data/atmo/tsar && sha256sum -c inputs.lock)
In the public repository: yes, beside this description.
Obtained: typed by hand (or OCR checked by hand) from a printed or scanned source.
From: N. Murayama, J. Meteor. Soc. Japan 40 (1962) 222-231, Table 1, pp. 226-227 (J-STAGE scan);
  typed by hand by the assistant (Claude) from the scans, under Xyphophor's review.
Checks: The printed velocities are recomputed from the transcribed times and distances and must
  agree (the build fails otherwise).
What could still be wrong: Only the numbers of the table are copied; a digit misread in a time that
  the velocity check does not cover would go unseen.
Collected: 2026-11-21
Licence: other — Journal of the Meteorological Society of Japan, free access on J-STAGE (©
  Meteorological Society of Japan); only the table's numbers are transcribed.
Sources:
- N. Murayama, Pressure Waves Produced by the Nuclear Explosion on October 30, 1961 - Preliminary
  Report, J. Meteor. Soc. Japan, Ser. II, 40 (1962) 222-231, Table 1 (pp. 226-227)
  <https://doi.org/10.2151/jmsj1923.40.4_222>
Evidence class (rule 10): measure
What would vindicate the flat model (rule 5): Laps that change with the station's position, as any
  echo would make them, or no third train after the first two.
Built by: fog build atmo-tsar
Generated files:
- src/data/presets/atmo-tsar.js
Code:
- tools/internal/build/tsar
- src/tabs/atmo/lambpool.js
- src/tabs/atmo/lambfig.js
Shown on the page:
- #tab-atmo/lp-h1
Notes: Laps are read at one station (third train minus first), so neither the site, the distance nor
  the explosion time enters. The first and third trains travel the same way round, so the wind along
  the path is not cancelled as it is for Krakatoa's two directions. The printed distances and
  velocities used the radius of 1962 and serve only to check the transcription.

Written by `fog build explain` from data/atmo/tsar/dataset.json and data/atmo/tsar/inputs.lock: edit those, not this file.
